# Connect to this skill library

A read-only MCP server over the skills in this directory. Any MCP client can
use it; nothing here is specific to one product.

{{SKILL_COUNT}} skills · {{PATTERN_COUNT}} patterns · {{UNIT_COUNT}} addressable units · {{TOOL_COUNT}} stdio tools (10 over HTTP by default)

---

## 1. Choose a transport

| Transport | When | What the client needs |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **stdio** | the client can launch a command | the command below |
| **Streamable HTTP** | the client accepts a URL | the endpoint below |

### stdio

```
python {{MAKER_DIR}}/src/skillmaker/mcp_server.py --skills-root {{SKILLS_DIR}}
```

Register it with your client as a stdio server whose command is that line.
Messages are newline-delimited JSON-RPC on stdin and stdout; the server writes
nothing else to stdout.

### Streamable HTTP

```bash
python {{MAKER_DIR}}/src/skillmaker/mcp_server.py --http 127.0.0.1:8765
```

| | |
| :--- | :--- |
| Endpoint | `http://127.0.0.1:8765/mcp` |
| Connect page | `http://127.0.0.1:8765/` |
| Health | `http://127.0.0.1:8765/health` |
| Authentication | none |
| Binds to | `127.0.0.1` by default |

The server is read-only: it cannot edit your files or the publications. It
serves the skills in this directory and answers questions about how they were
built. HTTP exposes only reading and derived tools unless started with
`--allow-maker-tools`. Enable this only for trusted clients. Document tools
accept regular Markdown files inside the repository, at most 32 MiB.

The reading corpus is frozen in memory at startup. Every reading response
carries a content-derived snapshot. Restart the server after a rebuild; a
running process continues serving its original edition.

HTTP limits: 1 MiB request bodies, 10-second socket read timeout, 32 concurrent
connections, 1024 sessions and a one-hour session lifetime. These are safeguards,
not a production hosting stack. External access requires a maintained reverse
proxy providing TLS, authentication and request/rate limits. Do not expose the
unauthenticated stdlib server directly to the internet.

A browser `GET` to the endpoint returns `405 Method Not Allowed`. That is
correct: it is a JSON-RPC endpoint, not a web page. Use `POST`.

---

## 2. Connect your client

Every MCP client has its own configuration syntax; follow its documentation.
The two facts a client needs are always the same:

- **stdio**: the command `python {{MAKER_DIR}}/src/skillmaker/mcp_server.py`
- **HTTP**: the URL `http://127.0.0.1:8765/mcp`

No credentials are involved. If a client offers only a local command, use
stdio; if it offers only a URL, run the HTTP server and point the client at it.

---

## 3. Check the connection

Ask the client:

> Use this server: report the corpus status and list the publications.

A working connection calls `get_corpus_status` and `list_publications`, and
reports a snapshot, a skill count and a pattern count. An answer that only
describes this file means the server is not connected.

Keep four kinds of evidence apart when you report the result:

| Layer | Claim | Evidence |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Configuration | a setting was added | the snippet you added |
| Connection | the client reaches the server | the tool list it received |
| Runtime | a request answers | one call's actual result |
| Freshness | the answer is current | the snapshot the server reported |

