## 4. Tools

### Reading

| Tool | Arguments | Returns |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `get_corpus_status` | — | snapshot, skill and pattern counts |
| `list_publications` | `limit`, `offset`, `kind`, `suite` | skills with their DocIDs |
| `search` | `q`, `publication`, `kind`, `suite`, `limit`, `offset` | units ranked by ID and title match |
| `get_table_of_contents` | `publication`, `patterns_only`, `limit`, `offset` | sections of one publication |
| `read_pattern` | `pattern_id`, `tier`, `section`, `limit`, `offset` | one unit, one tier, or one section |
| `read_section` | `publication`, `section`, `through`, `limit`, `offset` | a skill's entry point, a document, or `ID#anchor` |
| `backlinks` | `pattern_id`, `limit`, `offset` | units that mention an ID |

### Derived

| Tool | Arguments | Returns |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `route` | `pattern_id` | what to read first, and the section that checks a result |
| `entries` | `q`, `publication`, `limit`, `offset` | starting units for a described situation |
| `glossary` | `q`, `publication`, `limit`, `offset` | one-line meanings, taken from the units |

### Skill-maker

Available over stdio; opt-in over HTTP with `--allow-maker-tools`.
`verify_catalogue` uses the offline snapshot unless `offline: false` is explicit.

| Tool | Arguments | Returns |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `list_profiles` | — | the decomposition profiles this toolchain knows |
| `probe_document` | `path` | a document's kind, profile and unit count |
| `plan_decomposition` | `path`, `skill` | the artefacts a build would produce |
| `verify_catalogue` | `offline` | local publications against a publisher catalogue |

### Reading less

`read_pattern` accepts a `tier`:

- `contract` — the working sections only (problem frame, solution, conformance
  checklist, consequences, relations). Typically 35–60% of the bytes.
- `source` — worked cases, rationale, source comparison.
- `all` — the whole unit (default).

`reference/sections.json` records the line range and byte size of every section,
so a client without section-aware tooling can read exactly what it needs.

